<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luxembourg Crisis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1867</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Luxembourg Crisis of 1867 was a diplomatic confrontation between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> over the status of Luxembourg. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> negotiated to buy the grand duchy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-the-netherlands">William III of the Netherlands</a>, but Prussian and German opposition raised the threat of war. A conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> settled the dispute with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1867">Treaty of London</a> of 1867, which made Luxembourg perpetually neutral, withdrew the Prussian garrison and ordered the fortress dismantled.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luxembourg-crisis.jpg" alt="Luxembourg Crisis" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julien Then · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BismarckArbeitszimmer1886rest.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
